      "goal": "Elementary finite-sum and trigonometric-correlation verification: seek a genuinely independent proof of the sharp cyclic inequality, validate equality and constants by exact finite calculations, and audit exceptional cases without disguising spectral arguments as elementary independence.",
      "relative_weight": 0.5,
      "initial_prompt": "ROUND 1 ONLY — ELEMENTARY FINITE-SUM AND TRIGONOMETRIC-CORRELATION AUDIT.\n\nLet N >= 3 and let x_0,...,x_{N-1} be real with x_N = x_0 and sum_j x_j = 0. Define E(x) = sum_j (x_{j+1}-x_j)^2 and M(x) = sum_j x_j^2.\n\nObjective: Establish the sharp inequality E(x) >= 4 sin^2(pi/N) M(x), with equality exactly when x_j = A cos(2 pi j/N) + B sin(2 pi j/N). Seek a genuinely independent elementary proof based on finite sums, summation by parts, trigonometric correlation identities, nonnegative sum-of-squares decompositions, or another transparent finite-dimensional inequality, rather than simply restating DFT diagonalization.\n\nSuggested investigation: Introduce cyclic correlations R_m = sum_j x_j x_{j+m}, derive directly E(x) = 2(R_0-R_1), and investigate whether the zero-mean identity sum_m R_m = 0 and elementary trigonometric identities yield a sharp positivity certificate. Alternatively construct a direct finite-sum or sum-of-squares proof with explicit coefficients. Every proposed certificate must be proved algebraically, with correct indexing, signs, and boundary terms. Avoid assuming that positivity of a correlation expression is automatic.\n\nSharpness audit: Evaluate E(x) and M(x) exactly for x_j = cos(2 pi j/N) and x_j = sin(2 pi j/N). Establish their zero means and finite trigonometric orthogonality identities without invoking an unproved Fourier theorem. Show that all their real linear combinations attain equality. Explain how your argument rules out additional equality cases; do not infer the complete equality space merely from exhibiting extremizers.\n\nBoundary audit: Check N = 3 and N = 4 explicitly using elementary calculations. For N = 3, prove directly that E(x) = 3M(x) for every zero-mean vector. For N = 4, identify the equality vectors and exhibit a zero-mean vector with strict inequality. Include checks of cyclic indexing and the sharp constant.\n\nMethodological integrity: Explicitly distinguish a genuinely independent finite-sum proof from a disguised Fourier or eigenvalue proof. A constrained-optimization argument leading to the circulant eigenvalue recurrence may be useful as an alternative complete verification, but must be labeled as spectrally equivalent rather than genuinely independent. If no independent proof is obtained, present the rigorous partial identities and sharpness checks, clearly identify the missing step, and give an honest PARTIAL status instead of asserting an unsupported proof.\n\nDeliverable: A concise structured report with status, summary, findings, rigorous evidence, obstacles, and recommended next steps, approximately 700 words when feasible. Record promising elementary identities for a future stability analysis. This is exactly one authorized round. Do not use external sources, execute software, invent additional children, or start Round 2. Stop after the mandatory report."
    }
  ]
}
